{"causes":["The early space program's need for safe food for astronauts prompted the development of HACCP.","Rising incidents of foodborne illnesses in the mid-20th century highlighted the necessity for improved food safety measures.","Increased globalization of food supply chains created a demand for standardized safety protocols."],"effects":["The adoption of HACCP has led to a significant decrease in foodborne illnesses in various countries.","HACCP principles have become a regulatory requirement in many nations, influencing legislation and food safety frameworks.","The methodology has inspired the development of other food safety management systems and standards globally."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"food-safety-regulations-global","sourceName":"Food Safety Regulations Global","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"Food safety regulations worldwide have increasingly integrated HACCP principles since the late 1990s."},{"sourceSlug":"nasa-space-program","sourceName":"NASA Space Program","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"NASA developed HACCP in the early 1960s to ensure food safety for astronauts on long-duration missions."},{"sourceSlug":"foodborne-illnesses-outbreaks","sourceName":"Foodborne Illnesses Outbreaks","verb":"CAUSES","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"The rise in foodborne illnesses during the mid-20th century spurred the need for HACCP protocols."},{"sourceSlug":"global-food-supply-chain","sourceName":"Global Food Supply Chain","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"HACCP principles were integrated into the global food supply chain in the 1990s."},{"sourceSlug":"world-health-organization","sourceName":"World Health Organization","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points","targetName":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points","context":"The WHO endorsed HACCP as a standard for food safety management in the late 1990s."}],"places":[{"name":"Washington, D.C., USA","role":"Development Site"},{"name":"Geneva, Switzerland","role":"International Endorsement"},{"name":"New York, USA","role":"Global Food Safety Meetings"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: hazard analysis and critical control points","type":"Reference article","slug":"hazard-analysis-and-critical-control-points"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q279454","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazard_Analysis_Critical_Control_Point"],"tags":["methodology","Political & Polemical Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"hazard analysis and critical control points contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points (HACCP) is a systematic preventive food safety management system that identifies, evaluates, and controls hazards (biological, chemical, physical) significant to food safety throughout the food production process — from raw material production through processing, distribution, and consumption. HACCP was developed jointly by the Pillsbury Company, the US Army Natick Laboratories, and NASA in the late 1950s–1960s as part of NASA's requirement for zero-defect food for the manned space programme — astronauts could not risk food poisoning in space — and was first presented publicly by Howard Bauman (Pillsbury's food safety director) at the 1971 National Conference on Food Protection. The system is based on seven principles: identify hazards, determine critical control points (CCPs), establish critical limits, establish monitoring procedures, establish corrective actions, establish verification procedures, and establish documentation.

HACCP represented a revolutionary shift in food safety philosophy — from end-product testing (testing finished food for contamination, which is reactive, statistically limited, and economically wasteful) to process control (identifying the specific points in the production process where hazards can be controlled, monitoring those points, and taking corrective action when critical limits are exceeded). This preventive, process-based approach — with its emphasis on systematic hazard analysis and documented control — proved far more effective at ensuring food safety than the traditional inspection-and-testing model.

HACCP has become the international standard for food safety management — incorporated into the Codex Alimentarius (the UN/WHO international food standards body) in 1993, mandated by the EU for all food businesses from 1995 (Directive 93/43/EEC) and by the US FDA for seafood (1997), meat and poultry, and most recently through the Food Safety Modernization Act (FSMA, 2011). Its systematic, documented approach has influenced the development of ISO 22000 (Food Safety Management Systems) and of analogous hazard analysis approaches in pharmaceutical manufacturing, medical device production, and other industries where process safety is critical.","subjects":["Food Safety","Modern Era","Systems Engineering","NASA","Public Health","Food Industry","Regulatory Standards","Operations Research"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"HACCP transformed food safety practices globally, significantly reducing foodborne illnesses and influencing food legislation, affecting millions of people.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
